---
date added: 2026-10-13
title: Chicken and Dumplings
author: Alison Roman
source: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1019108-chicken-and-dumplings
tags: chicken, dumplings, stew, dinner
servings: 6
time required: 2 hours
---

Season @chicken thighs{2%pounds} (bone-in, skin-on) on both sides with @kosher salt{} and @freshly ground black pepper{}. Heat @vegetable oil{2%tablespoons} in a large #Dutch oven{} over medium-high heat. Working in batches if needed, sear chicken, skin-side down, until deeply golden brown, ~{8-10%minutes}. Flip chicken and continue to cook until it is browned on the other side, another ~{5-8%minutes}. Transfer chicken to a large plate, and pour off all fat into a measuring cup. (You should have about 5 tablespoons, depending on the fattiness of the chicken.)

Leaving all the browned bits in the &Dutch oven{}, return 2 tablespoons of fat to the pot. Add the @yellow onion{1%large} (diced), @celery{4%stalks} (chopped) and half the @carrots{6%medium} (about 1 ½ pounds, peeled and sliced ¼-inch thick). Season with salt and pepper and cook on medium heat, stirring to scrape up all the bits on the bottom of the pot. Cook until vegetables start to soften, about ~{4%minutes} or so. Return the chicken to the pot along with the @thyme{4%sprigs} and 8 cups of @water{8%cups}. Simmer, uncovered, until the chicken is completely tender and the liquid has reduced by about ¼, about ~{30-40%minutes}.

Transfer the chicken to a plate or cutting board to cool. Strain the stock (you should have about 5 cups; if you have less, you can add water to make up the difference) and wipe out the pot.

Heat the remaining 3 tablespoons chicken fat along with @unsalted butter{1%tablespoon} (plus more as needed) over medium heat in the &Dutch oven{} (if you don't have enough chicken fat, use enough butter to equal 4 tablespoons of fat). Sprinkle in @all-purpose flour{1/4%cup} and stir constantly until it's all a pale golden brown, about ~{2-3%minutes}.

Slowly whisk in the reserved chicken stock until no lumps remain (it will thicken considerably at first) and bring to a boil. Add the @leeks{2%large} (white and light green parts, thinly sliced) and the remaining carrots, season with salt and pepper and lower the heat to a simmer.

Remove and discard the skin and bones from the chicken. Shred the meat and add it to the pot. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the liquid is nicely thickened and the carrots and leeks are tender, ~{10-12%minutes}.

Make the dumplings: in a #medium bowl{}, whisk together @all-purpose flour{1 1/2%cups}, @baking powder{1 1/2%teaspoons}, @kosher salt{3/4%teaspoon} and @freshly ground pepper{1/4%teaspoon}. In a #small bowl{}, whisk together @buttermilk{3/4%cup} and @egg{1%large} and add to the dry ingredients, followed by @unsalted butter, melted{2%tablespoons}. Using a #wooden spoon{} or spatula, stir just to combine. (Do not overmix.)

Using a spoon, drop generous quarter-size dollops of the dumpling dough into the pot (they should just sit right on top; they will not sink), spacing them apart as much as possible (it's OK if they touch). Cover the pot and reduce the heat to low. Cook, undisturbed, until the dumplings are puffed and totally cooked through, ~{18-22%minutes}. (Test a dumpling by cutting it in half; it should look slightly biscuity, but with no raw bits of dough. If it needs more time, continue to cook.)

Remove lid and divide among bowls; sprinkle with @parsley{1/2%cup} (tender leaves and stems, finely chopped) and @chives{1/4%cup} (finely chopped), if using.
